   528	**Why one buffer set, not two generations (Claude review R1).** In the canonical loop, step t+1 needs actions from the forward on obs t, and that forward consumes the H2D copy of obs t. A second generation therefore buys no overlap. It would cost a second pinned buffer set, a full-row copy on every truncate, a new trainer API at every copy site, and a change to Isaiah's truncate plumbing (:991). The contract allows a synchronous baseline ("A synchronous-copy baseline satisfies this", W:docs/kaggriculture-contract.md, Environment). R1 makes that baseline explicit with the entry fence. Reopen double buffering only if a measured profile shows native step overlapping with device work would pay (Phase 6 Nsight), and then with the event design Codex drafted (preserved in commit `6ea11d2`, Codex's draft of this brief).
   529	
   530	Fence tests: (1) spy test: for `reset`, `step` and `truncate_envs`, `synchronize` is called before the native write, and a failed native call leaves all 35 buffers byte-identical; (2) CPU/unpinned path makes zero CUDA calls; (3) **pod-only DMA test** (runs in the early smoke or 6.1 receipt, not on the Mac): pinned buffers, enqueue a nonblocking H2D copy of every buffer behind a long device kernel, call `step`, then compare the device copy with a pre-step host clone. It fails if the fence is removed. That test is the L6 evidence; the spy test only proves ordering. This prevents host reuse hazards; **Task 0.2 was an Inductor GEMM overflow**, not this bug (W:cookbook/references/compiled-gemm-template-overflows-above-2-21-rows.md:16).
